g their biometric information  but there's a couple interesting  differences with facial recognition  so for one thing um facial recognition  uh almost requires no interaction so my  you know i can i don't upload my  fingerprint  until so onto social media and  i need to interact with a particular  device in order for my fingerprint to be  you know uploaded somewhere whereas with  facial recognition  we're very casual um with the way that  we  handle our face data we um treat it  differently than we do  other biometrics we're much less careful  and thus you can find someone's face  quite easily  and that can be included in any kind of  database um  and also as i'm walking through the  street you know a facial recognition  camera can capture me and it doesn't  necessarily have to notify me that i'm  now  you know captured and and my image  processed so there's a lot of  um challenges with respect to the lack  of direct interaction with facial  recognition tools that makes consent  very very challenging  um the other thing about facial  recognition is that um  starting in about 2014 with the deep  face  model um that was developed by facebook  uh deep learning  has been sort of the primary method of  of training models for facial  recognition and that means that  there's this enormous data requirement  in order for modern day facial  recognition tools to work effectively  uh what this means is that there's  pretty much databases of you know  millions of faces required in order to  train a really effective model to detect  particular  attributes or to you know a database  that is much larger than  um you know what you might need for  other sort of biometric tools using  other types of methods so  um as a technology it is quite  particular there are  characteristics of facial recognition  that position it to be especially  harmful and and challenging and  dangerous um and with respect to sort of  the policy  directions that i hope people take with  facial recognition  u