ic  um was a move for restriction so you  know there's particular  institutions like i mentioned police  departments as a good candidate  um but also you know uh there's been a  great work by the  um the citizen lab at university of  toronto that's done great work into the  use of facial recognition and  immigration processes and things like  that  so there's a lot of cases where perhaps  facial recognition is a tool that should  not be used ever  so um a lot of proposals for moratoriums  and bans  for particular individuals or  institutions  use of facial recognition in a  particular context that's deemed  you know too high stakes or two um too  sensitive  for that kind of technology to be used  um so yeah i think i think those are  like  three policy directions that i i often  um um speak about  um that i hope i hope canada definitely  embraces moving forward  thank you so much others who'd like to  comment patricia yeah i just uh that  that was  excellent thank you deborah i i wanted  to just add a little bit of a postscript  to  um the story about clear view ai and  and bring and comment a little bit on  the specific use case that you  uh presented rebecca um as  uh some of you may know um and and  deborah alluded to this uh it came to  light  the the the assistance that clearview ai  was providing to police services across  um canada came to light and it led to an  investigation  that is being conducted by the federal  commissioner along with quebec  bc and alberta are holding or conducting  a joint investigation interestingly  clear of uai  um pulled back  and uh ceased providing its services in  canada but the investigation is ongoing  because of  the importance of result of producing a  report  uh given the uh the stakes for for  canadians privacy so it'll be very  interesting to watch  i just want to say it's not the first  investigation on this specific use case  if you recall remember there were the  vancouver riots of  2011 following the stanley cup loss